+
+### Effective body modes
+
+By default, Praxis uses the body modes in Envoy's first `ProcessingRequest`.
+Some older proxies report the global mode even when a route sends bodies using
+a different mode; see [Envoy #39650]. For a processor whose routes use known,
+consistent modes, configure Praxis to match those existing routes:
+
+```yaml
+server:
+  processing_mode:
+    request_body_mode: streamed
+    response_body_mode: envoy
+```
+
+Each direction accepts `envoy` (the default), `none`, `buffered`, `streamed`, or
+`full_duplex_streamed`. Omitted directions continue to use Envoy's reported mode.
+An explicit setting also applies when Envoy omits protocol metadata entirely.
+Unknown and unsupported modes remain errors.
+
+These settings change Praxis's interpretation of the exchange. They do not
+send an ExtProc `mode_override` or modify Envoy configuration. A configured mode
+must match the mode the route actually uses; setting `full_duplex_streamed` here
+cannot turn a `streamed` route into full duplex. Use separate processor instances
+if routes require incompatible explicit modes.
+
+`streamed` acknowledges chunks incrementally, with the existing filter pipeline
+and end-of-stream validation. SSE response streaming is a separate HTTP concern:
+a route may stream an SSE response to the client while its extproc response body
+mode is `none`. This option does not establish end-to-end SSE delivery or quota
+accounting; qualify those on the complete serving path.
+
+[Envoy #39650]: https://github.com/envoyproxy/envoy/pull/39650
 
 ### Graceful shutdown
 
